{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790517087,"nonce":"4QM/do7ZfrdAccgr5njsqvzhTAg=","message":"Handover: this work moves to a cloud session, starting from 2bd8f3f.\n\nScope: the gap between wlift and LuaJIT (JIT on) on the benchmark kernels. LuaJIT -joff, stock Wren, Python and Ruby are shown for context and are not the target. This issue is the working log. ccc061d holds the older site-bench history (nbody, method_call, mandelbrot, fib), including what was already tried there.\n\nWhere it stands. CI x86_64 ratios (wlift / luajit, best of 10, from comment #1):\n- spectral_norm 4.97x\n- regex_redux 4.64x\n- method_call 3.29x\n- k_nucleotide 3.07x\n- reverse_complement 2.92x\n- fannkuch_redux 2.31x\n- pidigits 2.07x\n- fasta 2.03x\n- mandelbrot 1.34x\n- fib 1.23x\n- ahead: nbody 0.95x, binary_trees 0.19x\n- delta_blue has no Lua port.\n\nSince then 2f59d13 (a result guard on list reads) took spectral_norm -20% and fasta -11% locally. CI has not re-measured the ratios.\n\nBuild and measure\n- The bench job builds `cargo build --release --features llvm --bin wlift` against LLVM 21 (inkwell llvm21-1; CI installs it with .github/actions/llvm21; locally set LLVM_SYS_211_PREFIX). That is the build the release ships.\n- The LLVM top tier waits before it takes over (1bac040), so a short kernel can run entirely on the Cranelift optimized tier. Check which tier a hot function actually ran in (WLIFT_TIER_TRACE) before choosing which backend to fix.\n- A kernel prints `elapsed: \u003cs\u003e`: `./target/release/wlift bench/\u003ck\u003e.wren` and `luajit bench/lang/\u003ck\u003e.lua`. bench.yml takes the best of N for each language. Compare ratios on the same machine, run A/B interleaved, and take the best of 5; absolute times move between machines.\n- Diagnostic switches, all read-only and safe:\n  - WLIFT_JIT_DUMP: MIR per compile.\n  - WLIFT_LLVM_IR: LLVM IR. Add WLIFT_LLVM_PASSES=off for the IR before optimisation.\n  - WLIFT_TIER_TRACE, WLIFT_INLINE_TRACE.\n  - WLIFT_JIT_CODE_DIR: machine code to disassemble.\n  - WLIFT_TIER1=cranelift: Cranelift only.\n\nOrder of attack (by gap)\n1. spectral_norm. Each element does a static call (denominator) and a float divide. Comments #3 and #4 hold the analysis.\n   - On the LLVM tier, the loop that runs depends on compile timing and on whether the inliner versioned the loop. Sometimes it is the plain wren_call_2 loop with a generic divide and a boxed sum. Unresolved: which MIR shape produces that IR. Reproducer in #4.\n   - On the Cranelift tier, sum is carried boxed through the loop. The inner bound reaches the loop as a boxed block param, so j stays f64 behind a generic compare.\n   - Tried with no gain: dropping pass-through block params, rerunning TypeSpecialize, an Unbox(Box) fold.\n2. regex_redux: a static call, list reads and shift/and per base.\n3. method_call: warm-up, not steady state. See ccc061d #19 and #20, 56c3555 (record_call bookkeeping), b813673 (recompile only a cold loop's region) and 83be1ad (early top tier for top-level code).\n4. k_nucleotide: a Map with number keys. Also from #2: once dna[i] is guarded, counts[key] takes the list fast path, and its Map receiver deopts once. A non-list receiver there should call the helper instead of deopting.\n5. reverse_complement: list reads and writes from both ends.\n6. fannkuch_redux, pidigits, fasta (about 2x): profile after the above.\n\nRelated: a8a306d (per-call overheads), 685ff0e (MIR pipeline cost), 54840d6 (error poll after calls), 990178a (Cranelift compile time bounds warm-up). f1441ef is AOT, not this work.\n\nWorking rules for the repository\n- Commits: a short subject, a body only when needed, and a trailer `git-bug: \u003cfull 64-char id\u003e`. Never Closes/Fixes. No AI or tool attribution in commits, PRs or issues.\n- Code comments are terse: what and why. No measurements, no history of what was tried. That detail goes in comments on this issue.\n- Before each commit:\n  - `cargo fmt`.\n  - `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, in release and debug, with `--features llvm`, `--features aot`, and `-p wlift_lsp`.\n  - The two wasm lib checks: `--target wasm32-unknown-unknown --no-default-features`, and `--target wasm32-wasip1 --no-default-features --features aot_runtime`.\n  - `cargo test --release --lib`, and `--test e2e` both with and without `--features llvm`.\n  - Every bench/*.wren and bench/ash/*.wren must print the same output under the default build and under WLIFT_TIER1=cranelift.\n- A failure in tiered mode (a wrong value, Null where a Num belongs) is a regression, never a flake.\n- No shell scripts in tooling. Everything user-facing goes through the wlift and hatch CLIs.\n- Push nothing to main. Leave the work on a branch for review.\n\nIssues\n- Fetch with `git fetch origin 'refs/bugs/*:refs/bugs/*' 'refs/identities/*:refs/identities/*'`. After that, `git-bug bug show \u003cid\u003e` works.\n- Record findings and results as comments here (or on the specific issue) with `git-bug bug comment new \u003cid\u003e -F \u003cfile\u003e --non-interactive`.\n- The repository rejects any push that updates more than 5 refs. Push issue refs in groups of 5 or fewer.\n- If the session cannot push refs/bugs at all, put the findings in the branch's final summary, and they will be copied here.","files":null}]}